  "runner_up_case": "The strongest honest case for FALSE is an evidentiary-sufficiency attack, not an affirmative disproof. Every Q3 source in the file is weak: Wikipedia is tertiary; the Instagram and Facebook posts are undated social media; and the 26gambitchambers.com blog carries a publication date of 2026-10-06, which post-dates the 2026-10-01 as-of date and therefore cannot count as evidence as of today. No primary record has been pulled at all - no UK Companies House administration filing, no US bankruptcy docket, no dated wire report (Reuters/FT/BBC). The sources also disagree on mechanics ($50M vs $37M seized; 'announced insolvency proceedings' vs 'filed for bankruptcy'), a pattern consistent with an announcement hardening into a completed filing through repetition rather than verification. 'Announced insolvency proceedings' is materially different from 'entered insolvency proceedings': a company can announce an intention to appoint administrators while a rescue, sale, or creditor deal is still being negotiated. If the formal process was never commenced, or only a subsidiary (e.g., a US entity) petitioned while the UK parent continued, the flat claim 'Builder.ai entered insolvency proceedings in 2025' would be materially wrong or at best qualified. Identity, too, rests only on Wikipedia/Wikidata; confusion between the Engineer.ai Corporation entities (UK vs US vs Singapore/India subsidiaries) has not been excluded. On this record, FALSE (or at minimum UNVERIFIABLE) cannot be ruled out - though it must be conceded that the convergence of independent secondary sources makes FALSE the less likely verdict.",
  "would_flip_it": [
    "UK Companies House showing no administration or other insolvency filing for Builder.ai's UK entities (e.g., Builder.ai Holdings Ltd / Builder.ai UK Ltd) at any point in 2025.",
    "A search of US bankruptcy dockets (PACER/CourtListener) showing no Chapter 7 or Chapter 11 petition filed by any Builder.ai or Engineer.ai entity during 2025.",
    "Dated, credible reporting (Reuters, FT, BBC, TechCrunch) stating the May 2025 insolvency reports were wrong or retracted, or that the company was instead acquired, restructured, or continued operating.",
    "A primary company statement (LinkedIn post, press release, officer filing) confirming the company remained solvent and trading past 2025, or that proceedings actually commenced only in 2026, making the 'in 2025' claim materially wrong.",
    "Evidence that the insolvency narrative stems from confusion with a different namesake entity (e.g., a subsidiary or an unrelated Engineer.ai/Builder.ai company) rather than the main operating company."
  ]
}
